 ## Overview
-Neural Volumes (NV) is a method for synthesizing novel views of dynamic scenes by optimizing a deep 3D convolutional network to predict a discretized RGB$\alpha$ voxel grid and a 3D warp field [S1, S2]. It solves the problem of rendering semi-transparent, non-rigidly deforming objects (such as smoke or hair) from sparse multi-view video inputs without requiring explicit geometry or texture maps [S2]. It sets itself apart from implicit radiance field methods like [NeRF](52-nerf.md) by using an explicit voxel representation that allows for real-time inference and interpretable volumes, and from static voxel methods like [DeepVoxels](5540-endonerf.md) by incorporating a learned warp field to model motion and effectively increase resolution [S2].
+Neural Volumes (NV) is a method for synthesizing novel views of dynamic scenes by optimizing a deep 3D convolutional network to predict a discretized RGB$\alpha$ voxel grid and a 3D warp field [S1, S2]. It solves the problem of rendering semi-transparent, non-rigidly deforming objects (such as smoke or hair) from sparse multi-view video inputs without requiring explicit geometry or texture maps [S2]. It differs from previous ray potential works by modeling voxel transparency rather than occupancy probability, focusing on rendering rather than reconstruction [S2]. It uses an explicit voxel grid decoded from a latent code, avoiding the prohibitive computational cost of evaluating large MLPs at every ray-marching step [S1][S4, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Inputs:** A set of $K$ synchronized, calibrated RGB images from different viewpoints (typically $K=3$ orthogonal views) for each time instant [S2].
-*   **Outputs:** A rendered RGB image for a novel camera pose, and optionally a latent code $z$ representing the scene state [S2].
+*   **Inputs:** A set of $K$ synchronized, calibrated RGB images from different viewpoints for each time instant (typically $K=3$ orthogonal views) [S2].
+*   **Outputs:** A rendered RGB image for a novel camera pose, and a latent code $z$ representing the scene state [S2].
 
